// TEST: Diag Zip Distinct Index Types Same Extent (rejects)
// The NOMINAL half of the co-iteration agreement rule, isolated: same extent,
// different named index types. This is the invariant CLAUDE.md states --
// "Nat<LatIdx> and Nat<LonIdx> don't unify even at equal extent ... which is
// what makes A(i) bounds-safe by construction" -- and rank 1 was the hole in
// it. The rank->=2 product rule (`indexRecordsAgree`) has compared Tag all
// along, so `Array<.. like LatIdx, LonIdx> + Array<.. like DownIdx, LonIdx>`
// already refused; only the single-record path took the first operand's
// record unchecked.
//
// The name is genuinely in the IR to compare: `type LatIdx = Idx<8>` registers
// its record with `Tag = Some "LatIdx"` (registerTypeDecl's plain-index arm).
//
// The rule is name-PERMISSIVE on the anonymous side -- an untagged record
// co-iterates with either -- which index-types/253 pins. Two NAMES is the
// case that refuses.
// ERROR: BL3999
// ERROR-CONTAINS: operand 1 is indexed by 'LatIdx' and operand 2 by 'LonIdx'
type LatIdx = Idx<4>
type LonIdx = Idx<4>
let lat: Array<Float64 like LatIdx> = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0]
let lon: Array<Float64 like LonIdx> = [10.0, 20.0, 30.0, 40.0]
let bad = lat + lon
